# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Book 3 pass 2: full worked solutions for Appendix B. S[chapter][problem] = text.
Each solution: the idea, the formula, the numbers, then a check or the usual mistake."""
S = {}
S[1] = {
1: "Idea: the dopant sets the majority, mass action sets the minority, and conductivity follows the majority. p = ni²/n = (1.0×10¹⁰)²/1.0×10¹⁵ = 1.0×10⁵ cm⁻³. σ = q n μn = (1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(1.0×10¹⁵)(1000) = 0.1602 (Ω·cm)⁻¹, so ρ = 1/σ = 6.24 Ω·cm. Check: ten times fewer electrons than Worked Example 1.1 gives ten times its 0.624 Ω·cm. The usual mistake is to keep the hole term; it is ten orders of magnitude smaller.",
2: "Idea: ni carries the factor e^(−Eg/2kT), so the gap sits in an exponent. A wider gap means fewer bonds broken at any temperature and a much smaller ni. Junction leakage is fed by minority carriers whose concentration scales as ni², so it falls even faster than ni. Check with numbers: going from silicon's 1.12 eV to 4H-SiC's 3.26 eV cut ni by about eighteen orders of magnitude in Worked Example 1.3. The usual mistake is to think a wide gap only raises the forward drop; its main gift is low leakage when hot.",
3: "Idea: in intrinsic material both carriers are present in equal numbers, so both terms of σ count. σ = q ni (μn + μp) = (1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(1.0×10¹⁰)(1000 + 480) = 2.37×10⁻⁶ (Ω·cm)⁻¹, a resistivity of about 420 kΩ·cm. The hole share is 480/1480 = 0.32, about a third. Check: not ignorable. Dropping the hole term was fair in Worked Example 1.1 only because doping made p twelve decades smaller than n.",
4: "Idea: the unit cell holds eight atoms in a cube of edge a. N = 8/a³ = 8/(5.431×10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 8/1.602×10⁻²² = 4.99×10²² cm⁻³. Atoms per dopant: 4.99×10²²/10¹⁶ ≈ 5×10⁶, about five million silicon atoms for each dopant. Check the units: a must be in centimeters before cubing; 0.5431 nm = 5.431×10⁻⁸ cm.",
5: "Idea: doping moves EF away from midgap by kT times the log of how far the majority exceeds ni. Ei − EF = VT ln(p/ni) = 0.026 × ln(10¹⁷/10¹⁰) = 0.026 × 16.12 = 0.419 eV. EF lies 0.419 eV below Ei, toward the valence band. Check: p-type material must have EF below midgap; an answer above midgap means the sign was dropped.",
6: "Idea: 25 °C to 70 °C is 45 °C, five doublings at 9 °C each. ni rises about 2⁵ = 32 times, and a leakage proportional to ni² about 32² ≈ 1000 times. The full hand model ni ∝ T^1.5 e^(−Eg/2kT) gives 21.5 and 463, so the doubling rule is generous at this range because the per-kelvin rate (0.077 at 300 K) falls as T rises. The usual mistake is to forget the square for ni² leakage.",
7: "Multiplying the two Boltzmann expressions adds their exponents, and EF enters one with a plus sign and the other with a minus sign, so it cancels and leaves only Ec − Ev = Eg, a property of the crystal and the temperature alone.",
}
S[2] = {
1: "Idea: shallow donors are all ionized at room temperature, so n ≈ ND, and mass action gives the minority. n ≈ 1.0×10¹⁵ cm⁻³. p = ni²/n = 1.0×10²⁰/1.0×10¹⁵ = 1.0×10⁵ cm⁻³. Check: n p = 1.0×10²⁰ = ni². The usual mistake is to write p = 0.",
2: "Mass action fixes the product n p = ni², so the hole population falls but cannot reach zero. At ND = 10¹⁶ cm⁻³, p = 10²⁰/10¹⁶ = 1.0×10⁴ cm⁻³: ten thousand holes per cubic centimeter. They are rare, and they are exactly the minority carriers a junction later sweeps across, so they matter.",
3: "Idea: the larger dopant wins, and the net difference sets the majority. Neutrality: n + NA = p + ND. Donors exceed acceptors by 9.0×10¹⁵ cm⁻³, so the material is n-type. The small term is p, the minority; dropping it gives n ≈ ND − NA = 9.0×10¹⁵ cm⁻³. Then p = ni²/n = 10²⁰/9.0×10¹⁵ = 1.1×10⁴ cm⁻³. Check: p is eleven decades below the doping difference, so dropping it changed nothing. The usual mistake is to drop it before naming it, or to add the dopants instead of subtracting them.",
4: "Both regions have the same free-carrier concentration, but the compensated one holds 2.1×10¹⁶ ionized impurities per cm³ instead of 10¹⁶, and every one of them scatters carriers, so its mobility and therefore its conductivity are lower.",
5: "Idea: the sign of VH names the carrier, the size gives the concentration without needing the mobility. The negative reading means electrons, so the bar is n-type. In SI units, n = I B/(q |VH| t) = (0.5×10⁻³)(0.40)/[(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(1.0×10⁻³)(2.5×10⁻⁴)] = 5.0×10²¹ m⁻³ = 5.0×10¹⁵ cm⁻³. Check the units: t = 0.25 mm = 2.5×10⁻⁴ m, and m⁻³ divided by 10⁶ gives cm⁻³. The usual mistake is to mix millimeters and meters, which shifts the answer by a factor of 1000.",
6: "Idea: a resistor in sheet-resistance units is a count of squares. N = R/Rs = 1000/25 = 40 squares. At 1 µm wide the strip is 40 × 1 µm = 40 µm long. Check: any width works if the length scales with it; 2 µm wide needs 80 µm. Real layouts add a fraction of a square for each contact head.",
}
S[3] = {
1: "Idea: the Einstein relation ties diffusion to mobility through the thermal voltage, D = μ VT. Dn = 1000 × 0.026 = 26.0 cm²/s. Dp = 480 × 0.026 = 12.48 cm²/s. Check the units: cm²/V·s times V gives cm²/s. The usual mistake is to use VT in millivolts and get answers a thousand times too large.",
2: "Idea: diffusion current is charge times diffusion constant times gradient. |Jp| = q Dp |dp/dx| = (1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(12.48)(1.0×10²⁰) = 200 A/cm². Holes are positive, so conventional current points the same way the holes are spreading, down the gradient. Check against electrons: the same gradient of electrons would give a current pointing opposite to their spread, because their charge is negative.",
3: "Idea: with no gradient only drift is left, J = q n μn E. J = (1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(1.0×10¹⁶)(1000)(10) = 16.0 A/cm². Worked Example 3.1 gave 375 A/cm² from a one-micron gradient, about 23 times more. Check: a field of 10 V/cm is tiny; the steep gradients inside a junction are why diffusion matters there and drift dominates in the bulk.",
4: "Idea: resistance scales as 1/μ, and lattice-limited mobility falls as T^(−2.4). R(350) = R(300) × (350/300)^2.4 = 10.0 kΩ × 1.448 = 14.5 kΩ. Check: that is a 45 % rise over 50 K, about 0.9 % per kelvin on average, consistent with the 0.8 %/K slope at 300 K quoted in Section 3.4. The usual mistake is to apply the exponent with the wrong sign and predict a falling resistance.",
5: "Idea: τd = εs/σ. σ = 1/ρ = 0.1 (Ω·cm)⁻¹ and εs = 11.7 × 8.854×10⁻¹⁴ = 1.04×10⁻¹² F/cm, so τd = 1.04×10⁻¹²/0.1 = 1.04×10⁻¹¹ s = 10.4 ps. Check: ten times the resistivity of the 1 Ω·cm example gives ten times its 1 ps. Even lightly doped silicon neutralizes majority charge far faster than any circuit event.",
6: "Idea: at saturation the time is length over the velocity ceiling, t = L/vsat. t = 5×10⁻⁶ cm/1×10⁷ cm/s = 5×10⁻¹³ s = 0.5 ps. Check the conversion: 50 nm = 5×10⁻⁶ cm. The usual mistake is to use μE, which promises a shorter time than silicon can deliver.",
}
S[4] = {
1: "Idea: Vbi = VT ln(NA ND/ni²). NA ND/ni² = (1.0×10¹⁸)(1.0×10¹⁶)/1.0×10²⁰ = 1.0×10¹⁴. ln(10¹⁴) = 32.24, so Vbi = 0.026 × 32.24 = 0.838 V. Check: a hundred times more doping on one side than in Worked Example 4.1 adds VT ln(100) = 0.120 V to its 0.718 V. The usual mistake is to forget to square ni.",
2: "Only somewhat larger. W grows as the square root of Vbi − V. At zero bias the root holds 0.718 V; at −3 V it holds 3.72 V, about 5.2 times more, so W grows by √5.2 ≈ 2.3 times, not 5.2. Check against Worked Example 4.2: going from 0 V to −10 V raised the voltage across the layer thirteenfold and the width only 3.6 times.",
3: "Vbi = VT ln(NA ND/ni²), and ni grows steeply with temperature, so the argument of the logarithm shrinks faster than VT grows and Vbi falls, by roughly 2 mV per kelvin for typical dopings. Physically, the hotter crystal has more minority carriers on each side, so a smaller barrier already balances diffusion.",
4: "Idea: a symmetric junction splits the depletion layer equally, so the two-sided formula is needed. Vbi = 0.026 × ln(10³⁴/10²⁰) = 0.838 V. W = √[2 εs Vbi (NA + ND)/(q NA ND)] = √[2 × 1.036×10⁻¹² × 0.838 × 2×10⁻¹⁷/1.602×10⁻¹⁹] = 1.47×10⁻⁵ cm = 0.147 µm, half on each side. Check: ten times the doping of Worked Example 4.1 shrinks its 0.431 µm by about √10 = 3.2, slightly less because Vbi rose. The usual mistake is to use the one-sided formula, which gives 0.104 µm.",
5: "Idea: set Emax equal to the critical field and solve for the voltage across the layer. Vbi − V = εs Emax²/(2 q ND) = 1.036×10⁻¹² × (3×10⁵)²/(2 × 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 10¹⁶) = 29.1 V, so V ≈ −28.3 V, a reverse bias of about 28 V. Check: Worked Example 4.2 had 1.83×10⁵ V/cm at 10.8 V across the layer, and Emax grows as the square root, so 1.83×10⁵ × √(29.1/10.8) = 3.0×10⁵ V/cm. Measured breakdown of such a junction is nearer 50 to 60 V, because avalanche needs multiplication over a distance; the estimate is conservative.",
6: "Idea: the capacitance ratio is (1 − V/Vbi)^m. 1 − (−10)/0.7 = 15.29. For m = 0.5 the capacitance falls by 15.29^0.5 = 3.91 times; for m = 1.0 by 15.29 times. Check: the hyperabrupt profile buys a fourfold wider tuning range from the same 10 V swing, which is why tuning varactors use it. The usual mistake is to forget the sign: reverse bias makes V negative, so the bracket grows.",
}
S[5] = {
1: "Idea: well above IS the exponential term rules, I ≈ IS e^(V/VT). I = 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ × e^(0.65/0.026) = 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ × e^25 = 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ × 7.20×10¹⁰ = 7.20×10⁻⁵ A = 72.0 µA. Check: Worked Example 5.1 gave 10.5 µA at 0.60 V; 50 mV more is 0.83 decade, a factor of 6.9, and 10.5 × 6.9 = 72.",
2: "Idea: one decade of current costs VT ln 10. The rule says 59.9 mV. Exact: V = VT ln(I/IS + 1) gives 0.6585 V at 0.1 mA and 0.7184 V at 1 mA, a difference of 59.9 mV. Check: the rule is the exact ideal result once I is far above IS, and 0.1 mA is eleven decades above 1 fA.",
3: "Idea: in reverse the exponential vanishes and only −IS survives. I = IS(e^(−0.2/0.026) − 1) = 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ × (4.56×10⁻⁴ − 1) = −9.995×10⁻¹⁶ A ≈ −IS. The exponential is already below 0.05 % of the 1, so the current has saturated. A real diode leaks more because generation inside the depletion layer and surface leakage add currents the ideal law leaves out. The usual mistake is to report a positive current or to drop the −1.",
4: "Idea: at constant current the forward voltage drifts about −1.8 mV per kelvin. ΔT = 60 K, so ΔV ≈ 60 × (−1.8 mV) = −108 mV, and V ≈ 0.650 − 0.108 = 0.54 V at 85 °C. Check: the coefficient (V − Eg/q − 3VT)/T shrinks a little as V falls and T rises, so the estimate is good to a few millivolts. The usual mistake is to add the drift and predict a higher voltage when hot.",
5: "Idea: one decade costs n VT ln 10, so n = ΔV/(VT ln 10). n = 0.118/(0.026 × 2.3026) = 1.97, about 2. An ideality factor near 2 at nanoamp currents means recombination inside the depletion layer dominates. Check: n = 1 would need only 60 mV for the decade.",
6: "Idea: rd = n VT/I with n = 1. At 0.1 mA, rd = 0.026/10⁻⁴ = 260 Ω. At 10 mA, rd = 0.026/10⁻² = 2.6 Ω. Check: a hundred times the current gives a hundredth of the resistance. The usual mistake is to use V/I, the large-signal ratio, which gives about 6.6 kΩ at 0.1 mA, far from the slope.",
}
S[6] = {
1: "Idea: an abrupt junction's capacitance falls as the square root of 1 − V/Vbi. 1 − (−2)/0.718 = 3.786, whose square root is 1.946, so Cj = 20 pF/1.946 = 10.3 pF. Check: Worked Example 6.1 took 10 pF to 3.54 pF at −5 V; a smaller reverse voltage should cut less, and halving is less than a factor of 2.8. The usual mistake is to forget that V is negative in reverse.",
2: "Forward current stores minority charge on both sides of the junction, and the diode cannot block until that charge has been pulled back out or has recombined; the recovery time is the wait. A Schottky junction carries its current as majority electrons going over a metal barrier, so almost no minority charge is stored and the recovery tail is largely absent. What remains is the charging of its junction capacitance.",
3: "The resistor sets the current through the knee, about (12 − 5.1)/R; the junction only sets the voltage, and without the resistor the 12 V rail would try to force 12 V across a 5.1 V knee and destroy the part.",
4: "Idea: thermionic emission gives JS = A* T² e^(−φB/VT). With VT = kT/q = 0.02585 V at 300 K: JS = 112 × (300)² × e^(−0.6/0.02585) = 1.008×10⁷ × 8.3×10⁻¹¹ = 8.4×10⁻⁴ A/cm². IS = JS A = 8.4×10⁻⁸ A = 84 nA. At 1 mA, V = 0.02585 × ln(10⁻³/8.4×10⁻⁸) = 0.02585 × 9.39 = 0.24 V. Check: Section 6.4 found 0.34 V for φB = 0.7 eV; 0.1 eV less barrier is about 0.1 V less drop. The usual mistake is to use the pn-junction formula with ni².",
5: "Heavy doping, above about 10¹⁹ cm⁻³, thins the depletion layer under the metal to a few nanometers, so electrons tunnel straight through the barrier in both directions and the contact acts as a small resistor instead of a rectifying Schottky diode.",
6: "Idea: above breakdown a TVS acts as its breakdown voltage plus a small resistance. The dynamic resistance from the data-sheet points is (9.2 − 6.4)/65.2 = 43 mΩ. At 30 A: Vclamp ≈ 6.4 + 30 × 0.043 = 7.7 V. Check: 30 A is about half the rated 65.2 A, and 7.7 V lies about halfway between 6.4 and 9.2 V. In practice it reads somewhat higher, because the junction heats during the pulse and the breakdown voltage itself rises. The usual mistake is to use the 5.0 V stand-off voltage as the start of the line.",
}
S[7] = {
1: "Idea: the optimum load is peak voltage over peak current, and the power is half their product. Ropt = 40/0.50 = 80 Ω. P = (40)(0.50)/2 = 10 W. Check: against the 28 V, 0.20 A case of Worked Example 7.1, the voltage rose 1.43 times and the current 2.5 times, so the power should rise 3.6 times, from 2.8 W to 10 W. The usual mistake is to forget the factor of 2 and report 20 W.",
2: "Open an InP HEMT first. At 94 GHz with a small input signal the noise figure is the specification, and InP HEMTs give the lowest noise figure from about 30 GHz through W-band. Do not open a GaN power switch or a handset GaAs HBT: neither is a low-noise millimeter-wave part. A GaN limiter in front of the LNA can be useful for survivability, but the 0.4 dB deficit is an InP problem.",
3: "GaN's wide gap and high critical field let one HEMT structure block hundreds of volts, and GaN grows on large, cheap silicon wafers, so the same material serves a 50 V radar transistor on SiC and a 650 V switch on silicon. InP has a narrow-gap InGaAs channel built for 1 to 5 V and sits on small, fragile wafers that spread heat poorly, so it has no place in a high-voltage switch.",
4: "Idea: Class A power density is the swing times the current over 8. Vmax ≈ 2 × 28 = 56 V. Power density = (56 − 3) × 0.8/8 = 5.3 W/mm. Periphery for 25 W: 25/5.3 = 4.7 mm of gate. Check: at 1 A/mm Section 7.6 found 6.6 W/mm, and 0.8 of that is 5.3. The usual mistake is to use the supply, 28 V, instead of the peak, 56 V, which halves the answer.",
5: "The electrons of the 2DEG come from polarization charge at the AlGaN/GaN interface, not from dopant atoms inside the channel, so the sheet can be dense while the well holds almost no ionized impurities to scatter them; Chapter 3's falling mobility comes from those impurities.",
}
S[8] = {
1: "Idea: IS is inversely proportional to the Gummel number NA,B WB, and the transit time grows as WB². Halving WB doubles IS: 2 × 1.6×10⁻¹⁶ = 3.2×10⁻¹⁶ A. The transit time WB²/(2Dn) = (5.0×10⁻⁶)²/(2 × 10) = 1.25×10⁻¹² s = 1.25 ps, a quarter of the 5 ps before. Check: the square law makes thin bases fast, which is why RF transistors chase thin bases; the cost is a lower Early voltage and easier punch-through.",
2: "Idea: α = β/(β + 1) and IE = IC + IB. α = 150/151 = 0.9934. IB = 2.0 mA/150 = 13.3 µA, so IE = 2.0133 mA, equally IC/α. Check: α is within 1 % of unity for any useful β, which is why IE ≈ IC is a safe hand approximation.",
3: "Idea: VBE = VT ln(IC/IS + 1). At 0.1 mA: 0.026 × ln(10¹¹) = 0.026 × 25.33 = 0.659 V. At 10 mA: 0.026 × ln(10¹³) = 0.026 × 29.93 = 0.778 V. They differ by 120 mV because two decades of current at 59.9 mV per decade is 119.7 mV. The usual mistake is to treat VBE as a fixed 0.7 V and miss that a hundredfold current change moves it by more than a tenth of a volt.",
4: "β ∝ NE in the injection ratio, so tripling NE predicts three times the gain. A real device gains less: heavy doping narrows the emitter's bandgap, raising its effective ni² and therefore the backward hole injection, and it lowers the hole diffusion constant and lifetime in the emitter. Above about 10¹⁹ cm⁻³ the gain can even fall, which is one reason the polysilicon emitter and the SiGe base were invented.",
5: "In saturation the forward-biased collector junction lets excess minority charge pile up in the base and collector, and that charge must be removed or recombine before the collector junction can block; a transistor held in forward-active mode never stored that excess, so it has less charge to remove and turns off sooner.",
6: "Idea: solve the Ebers-Moll formula for βforced. With βR = 1, αR = 0.5 and 1/αR = 2. Set 0.026 ln[(2 + x)/(1 − x/100)] = 0.1, so (2 + x)/(1 − x/100) = e^(3.846) = 46.8. Then 2 + x = 46.8 − 0.468x, which gives 1.468x = 44.8 and x = 30.5. Check: Worked Example 8.3 found 67 mV at 10 and 121 mV at 50, and 0.1 V falls between them. The usual mistake is to forget the βforced/βR term in the numerator.",
7: "The Schottky diode turns on at a lower voltage than the collector-base junction, so it diverts the excess base current into the collector before that junction forward-biases deeply. Little minority charge is stored, so there is little to remove at turn-off and the switch recovers quickly.",
}
S[9] = {
1: "Idea: gm depends only on the current; rπ adds β. gm = IC/VT = 0.25 mA/26 mV = 9.62 mS. rπ = β/gm = 120/0.00962 = 12.5 kΩ. Check: a quarter of the declared 1 mA gives a quarter of 38.5 mS, and rπ scales as β/IC. The usual mistake is to compute rπ as VT/IB without noticing it is the same thing, then use the wrong β.",
2: "Idea: ro = VA/IC and gm ro = VA/VT. ro = 60 V/0.5 mA = 120 kΩ. gm = 0.5 mA/26 mV = 19.2 mS, so gm ro = 19.2 mS × 120 kΩ = 2310, which is 20 log(2310) = 67.3 dB. Check: VA/VT = 60/0.026 = 2308, the same number without the current, as Section 9.4 promised.",
3: "Idea: keep IE at 0.986 mA with a larger VB, so the same VBE change is a smaller fraction of the voltage across RE. RE = (4.0 − 0.718)/0.986 mA = 3.33 kΩ. A 100 mV fall in VBE raises IE by 0.100/3330 = 30 µA, which is 3.0 % instead of 7.8 %. Check: the sensitivity is ΔVBE/(VB − VBE), and the denominator grew from 1.28 V to 3.28 V. The cost is 2 V less headroom for the collector swing.",
4: "Idea: fT = gm/(2π(Cπ + Cμ)) with Cπ = gm τF + Cje. At 2 mA, gm = 76.9 mS. Cπ = 0.0769 × 10 ps + 50 fF = 819 fF. fT = 0.0769/(2π × 839 fF) = 14.6 GHz. Check: it lies between the 13.5 GHz at 1 mA and the 15.4 GHz at 5 mA of Worked Example 9.4, closing in on the 15.9 GHz transit-time ceiling. The usual mistake is to leave Cμ out of the denominator.",
5: "gm rises in proportion to IC while ro = VA/IC falls in the same proportion, so the current cancels from their product, which is VA/VT, set only by the Early voltage and the temperature.",
6: "Idea: with the emitter at signal ground the gain is −gm times the collector load. The load is 5.6 kΩ ∥ 10 kΩ ∥ 100 kΩ = 3.47 kΩ. Gain = −0.0385 × 3466 = −133. Check: Worked Example 9.5 with 100 Ω of degeneration gave −27.5; removing the feedback raised the gain about fivefold and tied it to gm, so it now moves with temperature and bias. The usual mistake is to forget the 10 kΩ load and ro in parallel with RC.",
7: "The base current flows through the divider's Thevenin resistance and drops a voltage there; if the divider current is ten or more times the base current, that drop, and therefore the change in base voltage when β or temperature moves the base current, stays small, so the bias point does not follow β.",
}
S[10] = {
1: "Idea: Cox = εox/tox, with tox the equivalent oxide thickness. Cox = 3.45×10⁻¹³ F/cm/1.0×10⁻⁷ cm = 3.45×10⁻⁶ F/cm². One µm² is 10⁻⁸ cm², so that is 3.45×10⁻¹⁴ F/µm² = 34.5 fF/µm². Check: half the 2 nm of Worked Example 10.1 doubles its 17.3 fF/µm². The usual mistake is to leave tox in nanometers.",
2: "Idea: in saturation ID grows as Vov² and gm as ID/Vov. ID = ½ × 200 µA/V² × 10 × (0.4)² = 160 µA. gm = 2ID/Vov = 2 × 160 µA/0.4 V = 0.80 mS. gm/ID = 2/Vov = 5 V⁻¹. Check: doubling Vov quadrupled the current but only doubled gm, so gm/ID halved from 10 to 5 V⁻¹. High overdrive buys current and speed, not efficiency.",
3: "Idea: compare VDS with Vov. VDS = 0.1 V is below Vov = 0.5 V, so the channel is not pinched off and the device is in triode, where it acts as a gate-controlled resistor of about 1/(μnCox (W/L) Vov). In bipolar vocabulary that region corresponds to saturation, the switch's on state. The usual mistake is to carry the word 'saturation' across between the two devices.",
4: "Idea: each decade of leakage costs one subthreshold swing of threshold. 120 mV/80 mV per decade = 1.5 decades, so the leakage rises by 10^1.5 = 31.6, about 32 times. Check: at the ideal 60 mV/decade the same shift would cost 100 times; a poorer swing softens the penalty but also gives a weaker on/off ratio for the same supply.",
5: "Once carriers travel at the fixed saturation velocity, the current is the channel charge per unit width times vsat, which contains no L; shortening the channel only raises the lateral field in a region where velocity can no longer rise.",
6: "Idea: the body effect raises Vth by γ times the change in √(2φF + VSB). Vth = 0.45 + 0.40 × (√(0.70 + 0.30) − √0.70) = 0.45 + 0.40 × (1.000 − 0.837) = 0.45 + 0.065 = 0.515 V. Check: Worked Example 10.4 found 0.637 V at VSB = 1.0 V, and the square root makes the first 0.3 V cost less per volt than the rest. The usual mistake is to omit the 2φF inside the root.",
7: "The fin is thin and fully depleted, and the gate wraps three sides of it, so the gate sets the channel potential almost entirely; the substrate has little electrostatic coupling to the channel, so raising the source above the body barely moves the threshold.",
}
S[11] = {
1: "Idea: each fin contributes two sidewalls and a top, W_eff = N_fin(2H_fin + W_fin). One fin: 2 × 42 + 6 = 90 nm. Three fins: 3 × 90 = 270 nm. Check: fins taller than they are wide get almost all their width from the sidewalls. The usual mistake is to count only the top, 6 nm per fin.",
2: "Idea: each sheet is gated on all four sides, W_eff = N_sheet × 2(W_sheet + T_sheet). Per sheet: 2 × (25 + 6) = 62 nm. Four sheets: 248 nm. Check: the thickness counts twice per sheet because the gate covers top and bottom as well as both edges.",
3: "Idea: DIBL is the threshold shift divided by the drain-voltage change. ΔVDS = 0.75 − 0.05 = 0.70 V, so DIBL = 50 mV/0.70 V = 71 mV/V. Check: well-controlled FinFETs report a few tens of mV/V and short planar devices above 100 mV/V, so this device sits in between. The usual mistake is to divide by 0.75 V instead of the difference.",
4: "In a thin channel every point lies close to a gate electrode, so the gate's field sets the potential throughout the channel, while the drain's field, which must reach in from the far end, finds little channel left to influence.",
5: "Idea: the same fin formula. One fin: 2 × 52 + 7 = 111 nm. Four fins: 4 × 111 = 444 nm. Check: at about 1 mA/µm that is roughly 0.44 mA of drive.",
6: "The width is set by the number of fins, each a fixed lithographic structure with the same height and width everywhere on the wafer, so the effective width comes only in whole multiples of the per-fin width.",
}
S[12] = {
1: "Idea: the reference current feeds one collector and two base currents. IOUT = IREF/(1 + 2/β) = 200 µA/(1 + 2/50) = 200/1.04 = 192.3 µA. Check: the error 2/β = 4 % is twice that of the β = 100 mirror in Worked Example 12.1. The usual mistake is to subtract only one base current.",
2: "Idea: in saturation a threshold difference becomes a current difference of 2ΔVth/Vov. σ(ΔI/I) = 2 × 2 mV/0.15 V = 0.0267 = 2.7 %. Check: a lower overdrive makes the same threshold mismatch worse, which is why current mirrors that must match are biased at higher Vov than input pairs.",
3: "Idea: Pelgrom's law, σ(ΔVth) = AVt/√(WL), solved for area. √(WL) = 4 mV·µm/0.5 mV = 8 µm, so WL = 64 µm², for example 8 µm × 8 µm. Check: an eightfold improvement over a 1 µm² pair needs 64 times the area. The usual mistake is to scale area linearly with the improvement.",
4: "Idea: each side runs at half the tail current, and the differential gain is gm RC. IC = 200 µA, gm = 200 µA/26 mV = 7.69 mS, gain = 7.69 mS × 20 kΩ = 154. Check: gm RC equals IC RC/VT = 4 V/0.026 V = 154, the voltage across one load resistor in thermal voltages. The usual mistake is to use the full 400 µA tail current for gm.",
5: "Idea: an IS mismatch appears as a VBE offset of VT ln(IS1/IS2). Vos = 0.026 × ln(1.02) = 0.026 × 0.0198 = 0.51 mV. Check: about 0.26 mV per percent of mismatch, as Section 12.2 found for 1 %.",
6: "Edge effects such as diffusion under the gate edge, corner rounding, and stress near the ends of a device scale with its perimeter, not its area, so one device four times wider does not carry exactly four times the current, while four copies of the same unit device reproduce every edge effect exactly four times.",
7: "Idea: a cascode multiplies the output resistance by the stacked device's intrinsic gain. Rout ≈ gm ro × ro = 40 × 100 kΩ = 4 MΩ. Check: forty times the simple mirror's 100 kΩ. In a bipolar cascode the gain would be capped near β ro.",
8: "Both stacked transistors must stay in saturation, so the output must stay at least two overdrive voltages, or a threshold plus two overdrives for the basic bias, above the rail instead of one, and that extra voltage comes out of the signal swing.",
}
S[13] = {
1: "Idea: stage gains multiply, and decibels are 20 log of the product. A₀ = 180 × 220 = 39,600. In decibels, 20 log(39,600) = 20 × 4.598 = 92.0 dB. Check: 45.1 dB plus 46.8 dB is also 92.0 dB, since decibels add when gains multiply. The usual mistake is to use 10 log, which gives 46 dB.",
2: "Idea: GBW = gm1/(2πCc), solved for Cc. Cc = 0.25 mS/(2π × 5 MHz) = 2.5×10⁻⁴/3.14×10⁷ = 7.96 pF. Check: Worked Example 13.2 needed 15.9 pF for 0.10 mS at 1 MHz; 2.5 times the gm and 5 times the bandwidth gives 15.9 × 2.5/5 = 7.96 pF.",
3: "Idea: the whole tail current charges Cc during a large step, SR = IEE/Cc. SR = 40 µA/30 pF = 1.33×10⁶ V/s = 1.33 V/µs. Check: the 741 has about 19 µA into the same 30 pF and slews about 0.5 to 0.6 V/µs, so twice the current gives about twice the rate. The usual mistake is to use the current of one input transistor instead of the tail.",
4: "Idea: SR = 2π GBW × (IEE/gm1), and for a MOS pair IEE/gm1 = Vov. SR = 2π × 10×10⁶ × 0.15 = 9.42×10⁶ V/s = 9.4 V/µs. Check: a bipolar pair would have IEE/gm1 = 52 mV and slew only 3.3 V/µs at the same GBW.",
5: "Idea: the second pole gm6/(2πCL) must sit at 2.2 × GBW = 6.6 MHz. CL = gm6/(2π × 6.6 MHz) = 4 mS/4.15×10⁷ = 96 pF. Check: Worked Example 13.4 allowed 72 pF for 1 mS at 2.2 MHz; four times the gm at three times the frequency allows 72 × 4/3 = 96 pF.",
6: "Each stage's gain gm ro/2 does not depend on current, because at fixed overdrive gm doubles while ro = 1/(λ ID) halves; but the second pole gm6/(2πCL) moves up with gm6, so a load capacitance twice as large leaves that pole where it was.",
7: "Idea: SR = IEE/Cc, and full-power bandwidth is SR/(2πVp). SR = 20 µA/10 pF = 2 V/µs. f = 2×10⁶/(2π × 5) = 63.7 kHz. Check: a 5 V peak sine at 63.7 kHz has a maximum slope of 2π × 63.7×10³ × 5 = 2.0 V/µs, exactly the slew rate. The usual mistake is to use the peak-to-peak amplitude, which halves the answer.",
8: "A JFET has much less transconductance per unit of current than a bipolar transistor, so it must run at more tail current to reach the same gm1 and hence the same GBW, and the slew rate IEE/Cc rises with that current.",
}
S[14] = {
1: "Idea: shot noise density is √(2qI). √(2 × 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 10⁻⁴) = √(3.20×10⁻²³) = 5.66×10⁻¹² A/√Hz = 5.7 pA/√Hz. Check: a tenth of Worked Example 14.1's 1 mA gives 17.9/√10 = 5.7 pA/√Hz. The usual mistake is to forget the factor of 2.",
2: "Idea: the collector shot noise referred through gm gives en = VT √(2q/IC). en = 0.026 × √(2 × 1.602×10⁻¹⁹/10⁻⁴) = 0.026 × 5.66×10⁻⁸ = 1.47 nV/√Hz. Check: a tenth of the current of Worked Example 14.2 raises its 0.47 nV/√Hz by √10. Lower current means higher voltage noise and lower current noise.",
3: "Idea: Ropt = en/in = rπ/√β. rπ = β/gm = 200 × 26 Ω = 5.2 kΩ. √200 = 14.1, so Ropt = 5.2 kΩ/14.1 = 368 Ω. Check: the β = 100 transistor of Worked Example 14.2 had 260 Ω; a higher β lowers the base-current noise and raises Ropt as √β.",
4: "Idea: en² = 4kTγ/gm. en = √(1.60×10⁻²⁰ × 0.667/4×10⁻³) = √(2.67×10⁻¹⁸) = 1.63 nV/√Hz. Check: ten times the gm of Worked Example 14.3 divides its 5.2 nV/√Hz by √10. The usual mistake is to use the 26 mV thermal voltage in place of 4kT.",
5: "Idea: below the corner the density is en,white √(1 + fc/f). At 2 Hz: 8 × √(1 + 200/2) = 8 × √101 = 80 nV/√Hz. Check: two decades below the corner, the flicker power is 100 times the white power and the density ten times the white density. The usual mistake is to scale the density by fc/f instead of its square root.",
6: "At 10 MΩ the input current noise flowing in the source dominates: a bipolar input's roughly 1 pA/√Hz becomes 10 µV/√Hz, far above any voltage noise, while a CMOS input's femtoampere current noise becomes only nanovolts, leaving the source's own 400 nV/√Hz thermal noise in charge.",
7: "Idea: Ropt = en/in. Ropt = 3×10⁻⁹/0.4×10⁻¹² = 7.5 kΩ. Check: at 7.5 kΩ the in Rs term is 0.4 pA × 7.5 kΩ = 3 nV/√Hz, equal to en, as the definition requires.",
8: "Idea: compare the three terms at Rs = 1 MΩ. en = 5 nV/√Hz. in Rs = 0.7 pA/√Hz × 1 MΩ = 700 nV/√Hz. Source thermal noise = √(1.60×10⁻²⁰ × 10⁶) = 126 nV/√Hz. The current-noise term dominates, by a factor of 5.5 over the source itself. Check: Rs is 140 times the NE5532's Ropt of 7 kΩ, deep into the region where current noise wins; a FET-input part belongs here.",
}
S[15] = {
1: "Idea: equal pull-up and pull-down strength needs the PMOS width ratio to make up the mobility ratio. (W/L)p/(W/L)n = μn/μp = 2.5. Check: with the book's working mobilities the ratio was 2.08; a larger mobility gap needs a wider PMOS. In FinFET processes strain narrows the gap and ratios near 1 to 1.5 are common.",
2: "Idea: tp ≈ CL VDD/(2Ion), the charge for half the swing over the on-current. tp = 3×10⁻¹⁵ × 0.75/(2 × 60×10⁻⁶) = 2.25×10⁻¹⁵/1.2×10⁻⁴ = 18.8 ps. Check: Worked Example 15.2 had 8 ps for 1 fF at 0.8 V and 50 µA; three times the load, slightly less voltage, and 20 % more current give 8 × 3 × (0.75/0.8)/1.2 = 18.8 ps.",
3: "Idea: Pdyn = α C VDD² f. Pdyn = 0.15 × 5×10⁻⁹ × 0.81 × 1.5×10⁹ = 0.91 W. Check: αC = 0.75 nF switched per cycle, times 0.81 V² gives 0.61 nJ per cycle, and 1.5 billion cycles a second gives 0.91 W. The usual mistake is to use VDD instead of VDD².",
4: "Idea: energy per transition scales as VDD². (0.7/1.0)² = 0.49, so 51 % of the energy is saved. Check: a 30 % voltage cut halves the energy, the reason voltage is the first power knob. The usual mistake is to report 30 %.",
5: "Idea: off-current falls one decade per subthreshold swing of extra threshold. 150 mV/75 mV per decade = 2 decades, a factor of 100. Leakage falls to 0.5 W/100 = 5 mW. Check: Worked Example 15.4 went the other way, one swing lower and ten times the leakage. The price of the higher threshold is speed.",
6: "Charging a capacitor to VDD through any resistance dissipates ½CVDD² in that resistance, because a smaller resistance carries a larger current for a shorter time and the integral of I²R is fixed; the resistance sets the speed of the transition, not its energy.",
7: "Idea: power scales with frequency at fixed voltage, energy per cycle does not. P = 5×10⁻⁹ × 0.64 × 1.5×10⁹ = 4.8 W, half of the 9.6 W at 3.0 GHz. E = 5×10⁻⁹ × 0.64 = 3.2 nJ per cycle, unchanged. Check: the 0.65 V, 2.0 GHz point of Worked Example 15.5 used 4.2 W and only 2.1 nJ per cycle, so lowering the voltage beats halving the clock.",
8: "Below threshold the gate delay, and therefore the cycle time, grows exponentially as the supply falls, and every off transistor leaks for the whole longer cycle, so the leakage energy per operation grows until it outweighs the CV² savings.",
}
S[16] = {
1: "Idea: charge sharing moves the bit line by the cell's excess voltage times Cs/(Cs + Cbl). ΔVbl = (1.1 − 0.55) × 15/(15 + 90) = 0.55 × 0.1429 = 78.6 mV. Check: Worked Example 16.2 gave 83 mV with a ratio of 20/120; a smaller ratio here is offset by the larger stored voltage. The usual mistake is to use the full 1.1 V instead of the difference from the precharge level.",
2: "Idea: a constant leakage drains the cell at I/Cs, so I = Cs ΔV/t. I = 15×10⁻¹⁵ × 0.2/0.032 = 9.4×10⁻¹⁴ A = 94 fA. Check: the result is in femtoamperes, the same order as Worked Example 16.2's 47 fA; a picoampere answer means a unit slipped.",
3: "Idea: ΔVth = Q/Ccg, so N = Ccg ΔVth/q. N = 0.2×10⁻¹⁵ × 0.5/1.602×10⁻¹⁹ = 624 electrons. Check: twice the coupling of Worked Example 16.3 at half the threshold shift gives the same 624 electrons.",
4: "Idea: n levels have n − 1 gaps. Two bits per cell is four levels and three gaps, so the spacing is 5 V/3 = 1.67 V. Check: TLC in a 6 V window had 0.86 V, and each extra bit roughly halves the spacing. The usual mistake is to divide by four.",
5: "Idea: the access and pull-down transistors form a divider on the node that holds zero. V = 0.9 × 0.2/1.2 = 0.15 V. Check: the node rose only 0.15 V, well below a typical inverter switching threshold near 0.4 V, so the read does not flip the cell.",
6: "Charge sharing with the large bit line leaves the DRAM cell at nearly the bit-line voltage, so its stored value is lost and must be written back; an SRAM cell's cross-coupled inverters actively hold their state through a correctly sized read and restore their own nodes.",
7: "Idea: the refresh commands must be spread evenly over the interval. 64 ms/8192 = 7.8 µs between commands, the standard tREFI. Check: the capacity does not enter, because each command refreshes a group of rows across all banks; larger devices refresh more rows per command, which makes each command take longer.",
8: "STT-MRAM is nonvolatile like flash but writes far faster at low voltage, has much higher endurance, and is built in the back-end metal layers of a logic process with only a few added masks, so it fits on advanced microcontroller nodes where embedded flash no longer scales.",
}
S[17] = {
1: "Idea: in the human-body model the 1.5 kΩ resistor sets the peak current. Ipeak = 8000/1500 = 5.33 A. Check: four times the 2 kV case of Worked Example 17.1 gives 4 × 1.33 = 5.33 A. The protection network must carry this for a time constant of 150 ns without its metal or junctions melting.",
2: "Idea: the stored energy is ½CV². E = ½ × 100×10⁻¹² × (4000)² = 0.8 mJ. Check: twice the voltage of Worked Example 17.1 stores four times its 0.2 mJ. The usual mistake is to use 100 nF and get an answer a thousand times too large.",
3: "Idea: the injected current develops I R across the substrate, which must stay below the emitter's turn-on. R ≤ 0.5 V/25 mA = 20 Ω. Check: Worked Example 17.2 allowed 60 Ω for 10 mA at 0.6 V; more current and a stricter voltage shrink the allowance. Twenty ohms means substrate contacts very close to the injecting device.",
4: "Idea: Black's equation gives a temperature factor exp[(Ea/k)(1/T1 − 1/T2)]. With T1 = 358.15 K and T2 = 378.15 K: (0.7/8.617×10⁻⁵) × (1/358.15 − 1/378.15) = 8123 × 1.477×10⁻⁴ = 1.20, and e^1.20 = 3.3. The life at 105 °C is about 1/3.3 = 30 % of the life at 85 °C. Check: Worked Example 17.3 found a factor of 4.0 for 20 °C with Ea = 0.9 eV; a lower activation energy is less temperature sensitive. The usual mistake is to use Celsius in the exponent.",
5: "Idea: ΔVth ∝ t^0.2, so ten times the time multiplies the shift by 10^0.2. 5 mV × 1.585 = 7.9 mV. Check: most of the ten-year drift happens early; the first year already delivered 63 % of it.",
6: "The protection clamp holds the pin at a few volts while the capacitor starts at kilovolts, so nearly all the voltage, and therefore nearly all of the I²R energy, appears across the 1.5 kΩ resistor in series.",
7: "Idea: the critical charge is roughly C V, converted to electrons. Q = 1×10⁻¹⁵ × 0.8 = 0.8 fC. N = 0.8×10⁻¹⁵/1.602×10⁻¹⁹ = 4990, about 5000 electrons. Check: a 5 MeV alpha particle frees 1.4 million pairs, so collecting under 0.4 % of them is enough to flip this node.",
8: "Idea: FIT rates add across bits. At sea level: 16 × 500 = 8000 FIT. At 10 km, ten times that: 80,000 FIT, which is 8×10⁻⁵ upsets per hour, one about every 12,500 hours, or 1.4 years. Check: for a fleet of 1000 aircraft that is about two upsets a day, so avionics memory needs error correction.",
}
S[18] = {
1: "Idea: L = √(Dτ). Lp = √(12.48 × 100×10⁻⁹) cm = √(1.248×10⁻⁶) cm = 1.12×10⁻³ cm = 11.2 µm. Check: a hundred times shorter lifetime than Worked Example 18.1's 10 µs gives a diffusion length ten times shorter than its 112 µm. The usual mistake is to forget that D is in cm²/s, so L comes out in centimeters.",
2: "Idea: E (eV) ≈ 1240/λ (nm). E = 1240/625 = 1.98 eV. Check: red light near 2 eV matches the 1.8 to 2.2 V forward voltage of a red LED quoted in Section 18.3.",
3: "No. A 1550 nm photon carries 1240/1550 = 0.80 eV, less than silicon's 1.12 eV gap, so it cannot lift an electron across the gap and passes through silicon unabsorbed. Silicon stops responding near 1100 nm. InGaAs on InP, with a gap near 0.75 eV, or germanium, near 0.66 eV, is used instead.",
4: "Idea: responsivity is η λ/1240 in A/W. R = 0.8 × 850/1240 = 0.548 A/W. I = 0.548 × 10 µW = 5.5 µA. Check: each 1.46 eV photon that is collected gives one electron, so 1 W of photons at 1.46 eV each could give at most 1/1.46 = 0.685 A, and 80 % of that is 0.548 A/W.",
5: "Idea: optical power is EQE times the electron rate times the photon energy. hν = 1240/630 = 1.97 eV. Popt = 0.35 × 0.030 A × 1.97 V = 20.7 mW. Electrical input = 0.030 × 2.1 = 63 mW. ηwall = 20.7/63 = 33 %. Check: the wall-plug efficiency is EQE times hν/(qVF) = 0.35 × 1.97/2.1 = 0.33. The remaining 42 mW heats the junction.",
6: "Idea: Voc = VT ln(IL/IS + 1), so ten times the light adds VT ln 10. ΔVoc = 0.026 × 2.303 = 60 mV, so Voc ≈ 0.68 V. Check: Worked Example 18.5 found 18 mV for a factor of 2, and ln 10/ln 2 = 3.3 times that is 60 mV.",
7: "Idea: the bandwidth scales as 1/√Cin. Cin = 70 + 5 = 75 pF. f−3dB ≈ √(10⁷/(2π × 10⁵ × 75×10⁻¹²)) = √(2.12×10¹¹) = 460 kHz. Check: 2.5 times the capacitance of Worked Example 18.6 divides its 730 kHz by √2.5 = 1.58. Reverse bias was worth more than half again in speed.",
8: "Negative feedback forces the inverting input to the same voltage as the non-inverting input, a virtual ground, so the photodiode's terminal voltage stays constant; its capacitance then carries almost no signal current and all the photocurrent flows through Rf.",
}
S[19] = {
1: "Idea: the drift-region design equations of Section 19.2. ND = εs Ec²/(2qBV) = 1.036×10⁻¹² × 9×10¹⁰/(2 × 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 100) = 2.9×10¹⁵ cm⁻³. W = 2BV/Ec = 200/3×10⁵ = 6.7×10⁻⁴ cm = 6.7 µm. Ron,sp = W/(q μn ND) = 6.67×10⁻⁴/(1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 1000 × 2.91×10¹⁵) = 1.43 mΩ·cm². Check: the square law predicts 51 × (100/600)² = 1.43 mΩ·cm². The usual mistake is to keep the 600 V doping and only change the width.",
2: "Idea: Ron,sp ∝ BV². Doubling the blocking voltage multiplies it by 2² = 4, from 51 to about 206 mΩ·cm². Check: the doping halves and the width doubles, and both raise the resistance.",
3: "Idea: Ron,sp ∝ 1/Ec³ at fixed voltage, permittivity, and mobility. A field three times higher gives 1/3³ = 1/27 of the resistance. Check: this cubic dependence is why the tenfold field of the wide-gap materials buys about a thousandfold, reduced by their lower mobility and permittivity.",
4: "Idea: the regenerative loop latches when α₁ + α₂ reaches 1. Here 0.45 + 0.40 = 0.85 < 1, so it is not latched. A gate current, or a rise in the anode current that increases both gains, must bring the sum to 1; once there, the loop holds itself on until the current falls below the holding current.",
5: "Idea: the IGBT's on-state is a fixed junction drop plus a resistance. P = VCE0 I + rCE I² = 0.9 × 40 + 0.015 × 1600 = 36 + 24 = 60 W. Check: the fixed-drop term grows linearly and the resistive term quadratically, so at this current they are comparable; a MOSFET would need below 37.5 mΩ to match it.",
6: "The IGBT conducts by flooding its drift region with injected holes, which must recombine or be swept out after the channel turns off, while a MOSFET's current is carried by majority electrons alone, which stop as soon as the channel closes.",
7: "Idea: I²R at the hot resistance. I² = 225. Si: 225 × 0.126 = 28.4 W. SiC: 225 × 0.075 = 16.9 W. GaN: 225 × 0.080 = 18.0 W. Check: each is 2.25 times its value at 10 A, and the ranking is unchanged; the SiC part still wins conduction because of its flat temperature curve.",
8: "Its body diode stores a large minority charge and recovers slowly, so when the opposite switch turns on and forces that diode off while it is conducting, the reverse-recovery current causes large losses and voltage stress in both devices.",
}
S[20] = {
1: "Idea: about 44 nm of silicon is consumed for every 100 nm of oxide grown. 20 × 0.44 = 8.8 nm. Check: the oxide grows partly into the wafer and partly above the original surface, 56 % above and 44 % below, because silicon dioxide is less dense in silicon atoms than the crystal.",
2: "Idea: Arrhenius law D = D0 exp(−Ea/kT). At 1373.15 K, kT = 8.617×10⁻⁵ × 1373.15 = 0.1183 eV, so Ea/kT = 3.5/0.1183 = 29.58, and D = 1.0 × e^(−29.58) = 1.42×10⁻¹³ cm²/s. Check: 100 °C hotter than Worked Example 20.1's 1000 °C raised D about tenfold. The usual mistake is to use Celsius in kT.",
3: "Idea: the profile spreads by about 2√(Dt). Dt = 1.42×10⁻¹³ × 3600 = 5.11×10⁻¹⁰ cm², √ = 2.26×10⁻⁵ cm, so 2√(Dt) = 4.5×10⁻⁵ cm = 0.45 µm. Check: an hour at 1100 °C moves boron almost half a micrometer, enough to wreck a modern junction; this is the thermal budget Section 20.2 warns about.",
4: "Idea: average concentration is dose over depth. 5×10¹³ cm⁻²/5×10⁻⁵ cm = 1.0×10¹⁸ cm⁻³. Check the units: 0.5 µm = 5×10⁻⁵ cm, and cm⁻² over cm gives cm⁻³.",
5: "Idea: R = k1 λ/NA. R = 0.3 × 13.5/0.33 = 12.3 nm. Check: k1 = 0.4 gave 16.4 nm in Worked Example 20.3, so a more aggressive process factor buys a quarter less pitch on the same tool.",
6: "193 nm immersion lithography could not print features below about 40 nm half-pitch in one exposure, and EUV tools were years late reaching production, so the industry split each fine layer into two to four aligned exposures, at the cost of more masks, more steps, and tighter overlay.",
7: "Idea: Poisson yield Y = e^(−A D0). A D0 = 2.0 × 0.2 = 0.4, so Y = e^(−0.4) = 0.67. Check: on average 0.4 defects land on each die, and the fraction of dice with none is 67 %.",
8: "Yield falls exponentially with area, so each small die has a much higher chance of being defect-free, and the defective small dice are discarded individually before assembly instead of one defect scrapping a whole large die.",
}
S[21] = {
1: "Idea: the film is half an acoustic wavelength thick, t = v/(2f). t = 11,350/(2 × 3.5×10⁹) = 1.62×10⁻⁶ m = 1.62 µm. Check: Worked Example 21.1 gave 2.32 µm at 2.45 GHz, and 2.32 × 2.45/3.5 = 1.62. Real films are thinner still, because the electrodes add mass.",
2: "Idea: the electrode period is one acoustic wavelength, and a finger is a quarter of it. λ = v/f = 4000/2×10⁹ = 2 µm, so the finger width is 0.5 µm. Check: half the 1 µm finger of a 1 GHz device, as Section 21.4 gave; at 5 GHz the fingers would be 0.2 µm, the reason SAW yields to BAW at high frequency.",
3: "Idea: (fp − fs)/fp = (4/π²) kt². The fraction is 0.10 × 4/π² = 0.0405, so fp = 3.5/(1 − 0.0405) = 3.648 GHz and the spacing is 148 MHz. Check: the shortcut 0.0405 × 3.5 GHz = 142 MHz uses fs in place of fp and is 4 % low. The usual mistake is to take kt² itself, 10 %, as the fractional spacing.",
4: "Idea: fp = fs √(1 + Cm/C0). fp/fs = √1.05 = 1.0247, so the parallel resonance sits 2.5 % above the series one. Check: Worked Example 21.2 had Cm/C0 = 0.055 for a 2.7 % spacing.",
5: "Sound in these materials travels tens of thousands of times slower than light, so at the same frequency its wavelength is tens of thousands of times shorter, and a resonator sized in acoustic half-wavelengths is micrometers instead of centimeters.",
6: "Scandium softens the lattice and raises the piezoelectric response, which raises the coupling kt², and the coupling sets the spacing between the series and parallel resonances that bounds a ladder filter's bandwidth.",
7: "Idea: the spacing is about (4/π²) kt² of the frequency. 0.0263 × 1900 MHz = 50 MHz. Check: the PCS band near 1.9 GHz needs 60 MHz plus guard band per direction, so a pure AlN ladder is marginal there too; band filters use careful design or higher-coupling films.",
8: "At 24 % fractional bandwidth it is roughly ten times wider than the resonance spacing a BAW film provides, so a ladder cannot pass it; laterally excited resonators in lithium niobate, lumped LC filters, or splitting the band are used instead.",
}
S[22] = {
1: "Idea: n is the measured voltage per decade divided by the ideal 59.9 mV. n = 0.06/(0.026 × ln 10) = 0.06/0.0599 = 1.00. Check: one decade of current for 60 mV is the ideal diffusion law, so this diode sits in its ideal mid-current window. The usual mistake is to use log base 10 without the ln 10 factor and get n = 2.3.",
2: "Idea: Pelgrom's law, σ(ΔVt) = AVt/√(WL). WL = 0.5 × 0.2 = 0.1 µm², √0.1 = 0.316 µm, so σ = 3/0.316 = 9.5 mV. Check: a tenth of the area of a 1 µm² pair gives √10 = 3.16 times its 3 mV.",
3: "Idea: solve Pelgrom's law for area. √(WL) = AVt/σ = 3 mV·µm/1 mV = 3 µm, so WL = 9 µm², for example 3 µm × 3 µm. Check: a threefold improvement over a 1 µm² pair needs nine times the area.",
4: "Idea: a Gaussian keeps 1 − 2Q(3) inside ±3σ. 1 − 2 × 1.35×10⁻³ = 0.9973, so 99.73 % of parts lie inside. Check: 0.27 % outside, about one part in 370.",
5: "The simulator solves the circuit by Newton iteration, which needs continuous currents and continuous derivatives; a jump or a kink between regions makes the iteration oscillate or fail to converge, and it also gives wrong small-signal values such as gm at the region boundary.",
6: "A memory has millions or billions of identical cells and all of them must work, so the failure probability of each cell must be extremely small, below about 10⁻⁸ for a 128 Mbit array, which is near 5.7σ; 3σ would leave hundreds of thousands of failing cells.",
7: "Idea: a two-sided spec at ±3σ fails 2Q(3). 2 × 1.35×10⁻³ = 2.7×10⁻³ = 0.27 %, about 2700 parts per million. Check: Problem 4 found 99.73 % inside, the complement.",
8: "Idea: σ ∝ 1/√(WL), so halving σ needs √(WL) doubled. Four times the gate area halves σ. Check: Problem 3 found nine times the area for a third of the σ, the same square law.",
}
